#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
receive_watch - GhostSpiral companion: watch a receive address until paid
=========================================================================
Closes the one manual gap in the receiver flow. create_receive_wallet prints
"3. Wait for sender's BTC to arrive and ThorChain to convert to XMR" and then
leaves the operator refreshing something by hand. This waits for them, tells
them when the money is really theirs, and then asks what to do with it.

    receive_watch --receive-wallet wallet_ab12.json --pairs thor_pairs.json \
                  --tor-proxy socks5h://127.0.0.1:9050

WHAT IT WATCHES, AND WHY THAT SIDE
----------------------------------
The sender pays BITCOIN, so the obvious design is to watch the BTC deposit
address. This tool deliberately does NOT, and will not be extended to:

  * Asking mempool.space / blockstream / any explorer "has this address been
    paid?" tells a party that did not previously know it that SOMEONE cares
    about that address, at that moment, repeatedly, for as long as you wait.
    Tor hides who is asking; it does not un-ask the question. The polling
    pattern itself then times the arrival for that party.
  * The BTC leg is not the event that matters anyway. BTC landing in a
    ThorChain vault is not money you control -- the swap can still fail, be
    refunded, or under-deliver. The event worth waiting for is XMR arriving,
    confirmed and unlocked, on YOUR subaddress.

So the watch runs entirely against YOUR OWN monero-wallet-rpc: the balance of
one subaddress of a wallet you already hold. On the normal localhost setup
that is a loopback socket and reaches no third party at all; the daemon behind
it syncs over Tor exactly as it already did. Watching therefore adds ZERO new
disclosure to anyone -- which is the only reason it is safe to poll for hours.

The swap provider is a different case: it already created the BTC-deposit ->
XMR-destination binding and cannot un-know it, so asking it about your own
swap discloses nothing new. That is still not done here, because it is not
needed -- the XMR side answers the question authoritatively.

OPSEC hardening:
  - Tor verified ONCE at start, never per poll (a check.torproject.org request
    on every tick would be a far louder pattern than the thing being watched)
  - Poll interval JITTERED, so a remote/observed RPC sees no fixed heartbeat
  - Amounts never enter the integrity log (they are the most linkable value in
    the whole pipeline); only state transitions are chained
  - Addresses scrubbed in the log; printed in full to the terminal, because
    the operator has to read them to use them
  - Spends NOTHING, ever. It reports and prints the command it would suggest;
    arming and running a spend stays where the rest of the toolchain puts it.
"""
from __future__ import annotations
import argparse, re, secrets, shlex, sys, time
from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_DOWN
from pathlib import Path

